  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/8d898ff9-ba52-4b5c-9169-bc83c2771057-image.png\" style=\"width: 550px; height: 333px;\" /><br /><br />In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, the central atom has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;bond pairs and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> lone pairs. Hence, the electrons will enter a trigonal bipyramidal geometry. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> lone pairs will take up equatorial positions giving the molecule a T-shape. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Xe</mi></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>XeOF</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math> bond pairs and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math> lone pair. The electrons will take up square bipyramidal geometry, but due to a lone pair, the shape of the molecule will be square pyramidal.",
